From: Macron <macron@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] opengl32: Queue calls that return nothing and replay them in
 one Unix call.

Every OpenGL call goes from opengl32's Windows side to its Unix side.
On macOS under Rosetta that transition costs about 14 µs, two Mach
traps to switch %gs and the MXCSR save and restore, far more than most
calls: a Direct3D 9 game through wined3d makes about nine calls a draw
and thousands of draws a frame.

Queue, per thread, the calls that return nothing and whose arguments
are values or point to data whose size is known at the call (buffer
uploads, uniforms, matrices, fixed-size vectors), with that data
copied, and replay the queue in one Unix call before any other call
reaches the Unix side. The Unix side replays each call through the
entry point it would have reached directly, so order and behaviour are
unchanged, glGetError included. Draws are queued only while no vertex
array is in client memory, elements draws only while an element array
buffer is bound. WINE_OPENGL_BATCH=0 turns it off.

Measured on an Apple M1 virtual machine with Apple's software renderer:
queued calls cost 0.13 to 0.27 µs instead of 13 to 19 µs, and a 32-bit
Direct3D 9 program drawing 3,900 one-pixel sprites went from 1.6 to 23
frames a second.

batch_table.h is generated by Macron's runtime/wine/gl-batch-table.py
from unixlib.h and unix_thunks.h.
